Do not take the echo for the near end's talk for a second after the second search starts again

The second search's filters start again (a partial MatchedFilter::Reset)
after half a second of a steady delay, which they only reach when the
capture is the echo: with the near end talking, their delay never stays
the same that long. After 8 to 10 s of the echo alone, the ratio of
capture to render still comes often more than 8 dB above its lowest
fifth, the capture is taken for the near end's talk, and the
suppressor's floor (AecState::NearTalkGainFloor) lets the echo through
at 0.5 until the usual search finds the delay, about a second later.

For a second after the first such partial reset of a stretch of the echo
alone once the second search's delay is reliable, do not count the
capture as the near end's talk. The partial resets that follow, every
half second or so while that delay stays (the search's aggregator keeps
it), do not start that second again, so the near end talking again after
a pause keeps its floor. The stretch is taken as over, and the next
partial reset starts the second again, once 3 s pass without a partial
reset (the delay did not stay: the near end talked), or with a full
reset. Within a stretch of the echo alone, partial resets mostly come
0.9 to 2.3 s apart; after the near end talks for 2 s or more, the next
one almost always comes more than 3 s after the last. Before, the
partial reset cleared what the filters knew of that talk, which also
stopped the floor, for 5 s and without keeping that the delay was found
in the near end's talk; now only the floor stops, and only for that
second.

The second ends at once when the ratio of capture to render comes more
than 6 dB above the highest it reached in the half second before the
partial reset (the echo alone, as the delay was steady then): the near
end talking again, which goes over it at once, keeps its floor, while
the echo, made by the render the ratio is taken against, stays under
it.

A unit test: after the first partial reset with the delay found, the
near end's talk is not taken for 250 updates, then it is; before the
delay is found, a partial reset changes nothing; the partial resets that
follow within 3 s leave the talk taken; one that comes more than 3 s
after the last starts the second again, and the next does not; a
capture 5 dB over the loudest of the half second before the reset does
not end the second, 10 dB over it ends it within 2 updates and the talk
is taken from then on; after a full reset, the first partial reset once
the delay is reliable again starts the second again.

The ceiling the second is ended over is the highest ratio recorded in the
last half second of updates (125) before the partial reset, not the highest
of the last 125 ratios recorded: each ratio keeps the update it was recorded
at. With the far end quiet for part of that half second (its updates, weak
render or a silent capture, are left out of the ratios), the last 125 ratios
reached back into the near end's talk before it; that talk set the ceiling,
the near end talking again stayed under it, and its floor was gone for up to
the whole second. When no ratio was recorded in that half second, there is
nothing to tell the echo from the talk by: the reset does not start the
second and leaves it for the next partial reset of the stretch.

Two more unit tests: with the far end quiet for most of the half second
before the reset and the near end's talk just before it, the echo alone is
still not taken for talk after the reset, and the near end talking again
ends the second within 2 updates; a full reset starts a new stretch at once,
so the first partial reset with the delay reliable after it starts the
second although no 3 s passed between partial resets, while without the
full reset the same partial reset does not.

